---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const title = 'The Story Behind CardHearth';
const description =
  "Why I built CardHearth — a free, no-nonsense home for classic card and puzzle games, where every Sudoku is solvable by logic and every Mahjong board is guaranteed clearable, with no ads in your face.";

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    name: 'The Story Behind CardHearth',
    url: 'https://cardhearth.com/about/',
    description,
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'CardHearth',
      url: 'https://cardhearth.com/',
      logo: 'https://cardhearth.com/logo.png',
      founder: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Jared' },
    },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'CardHearth', item: 'https://cardhearth.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'About', item: 'https://cardhearth.com/about/' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <main class="mx-auto flex min-h-dvh w-full max-w-2xl flex-col px-4">
    <nav class="breadcrumb pt-6" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">CardHearth</a>
      <span class="breadcrumb-sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
      <span aria-current="page">About</span>
    </nav>

    <header class="pt-4 pb-2">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">The story behind CardHearth</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg opacity-80">
        A free home for classic games, built on one stubborn idea: the game should never cheat you.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="prose py-4">
      <p>
        Hi — I'm Jared. I work in aerospace, and on the side I build websites. CardHearth started
        the way a lot of side projects do: I went looking for a tired, neglected corner of the
        web that I could honestly make better. Free online solitaire and puzzles turned out to be
        exactly that. The games are timeless and beloved, but so many of the places to play them
        felt like a chore — slow, cluttered, and weirdly hostile to the person actually trying to
        play.
      </p>

      <h2>The thing that finally bugged me enough to build it</h2>
      <p>
        Two things, really. The first was <strong>unsolvable puzzles</strong>. There are few
        things more deflating than grinding away at a "hard" Sudoku — especially with a win streak
        on the line — only to discover it needed a lucky guess, or sitting down to a Mahjong board
        that physically cannot be cleared no matter how perfectly you play. That's not difficulty.
        That's the game wasting your time. So I made fairness the foundation of CardHearth:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Every Sudoku is solvable by pure logic — no guessing, ever.</strong> Each puzzle
          is generated and then checked by a solver before it's ever served; if it can't be cracked
          by reasoning alone, it doesn't ship. Difficulty just means <em>which</em> techniques you'll
          need, not how lucky you have to be.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Every Mahjong board is guaranteed solvable.</strong> Deals are built in reverse —
          laying down pairs in the opposite of play order — so a winning path is mathematically
          guaranteed to exist. Get stuck? That's what unlimited undo is for.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>And the ads. So many ads.</h2>
      <p>
        The second thing was the assault. You know the pages — you click "play," and the screen
        fills with blinking banners, a video that autoplays in the corner, and a cookie wall
        stacked on a newsletter pop-up, all before you can see a single card. I hate that. A cozy
        game should feel calm. CardHearth loads fast, puts the game first, and never buries the
        thing you came for. No sign-up, no download, and your stats and streaks just save on your
        own device.
      </p>

      <h2>What it is now</h2>
      <p>
        CardHearth is <strong>11 classic games</strong> and growing — Klondike, Spider and FreeCell
        solitaire, plus Pyramid, TriPeaks and Golf; and on the puzzle side Sudoku, Mahjong,
        Minesweeper and 2048. There's a <a href="/daily-challenge/">Daily Challenge</a> that rotates
        through every game (the same for everyone, with a full-year calendar so you can go back and
        finish days you missed), shareable numbered deals, achievements, and a pile of small
        comforts: dark mode, card backs, sounds you can switch off, and unlimited undo everywhere.
        If you want to get better, there are <a href="/guides/">strategy guides and rules</a> for
        every game.
      </p>

      <h2>Who it's for</h2>
      <p>
        Honestly? Anyone who loves a cozy game and just wants the best possible version of it —
        whether that's five quiet minutes with a coffee or a daily streak you're quietly proud of.
        If that's you, you're who I'm building this for, and I want your time here to feel
        genuinely good.
      </p>

      <h2>Where it's going</h2>
      <p>
        More games, for one — the structure was built so dominoes, board games and other classics
        can join the card and puzzle families over time. The dream is simple: become the place
        people think of first when they want to play a classic game online, fairly and without the
        nonsense. If a few million people a month end up making CardHearth part of their day, I'll
        consider that a very good outcome.
      </p>

      <p>
        Thanks for being here. If you have a game you'd love to see, or something I've gotten wrong,
        I genuinely want to hear it — <a href="mailto:hello@cardhearth.com">hello@cardhearth.com</a>.
      </p>

      <p class="opacity-75">— Jared, CardHearth</p>
    </article>

    <p class="py-6">
      <a class="guide-cta" href="/">▶ Browse all games</a>
    </p>

    <Footer />
  </main>
</Layout>
